From command-prompt:
-XX:+PrintStringTableStatistics
-XX:+PrintSymbolTableStatistics
Note : These Strings are loaded by the core java
 
Try with application:
-XX:+PrintStringTableStatistics 

Now, Try with:
-XX:+PrintStringTableStatistics -XX:StringTableSize=120121




1. SymbolTable

The SymbolTable stores JVM internal symbols such as:

Class names
Method names
Field names
JVM identifiers


2. Shared Symbol Table

CDS (Class Data Sharing)

JVM shares preloaded symbols across JVM instances to improve startup performance.

Contains many standard JDK classes and symbols.


3. StringTable

The StringTable stores interned Java strings.


Performance Interpretation
==========================

Your JVM statistics look healthy.

Good signs:

Very low bucket occupancy
Very low collisions
Small maximum bucket size
Good hash distribution

This means:

Fast symbol lookup
Fast string lookup
No sign of hash table overload


Why JVM Uses These Tables
==========================

Without them:

Duplicate strings waste memory
Class loading becomes slower
Method lookup slower

These tables improve:

Memory efficiency
JVM startup
Runtime performance
Garbage collection efficiency


When Problems Happen
====================

If tables become overloaded:

Large collisions
Slow lookups
Increased GC pressure

Symptoms:

Maximum bucket size very high
Average bucket size large

Sometimes JVM tuning needed:

-XX:StringTableSize=1000003





